ion/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=5-3t,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(0)=5,\\,p(2)=-1,\\,p(3)=-4.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(0)p(0)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(0)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+1+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{42}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-97425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "032485b9c04d38c8796bb8cb7044f0da734fa798c5bcd90a61945b524ddeedac"} {"question" "q-97425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-97425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{11}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "\\sqrt{42}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-97425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{42}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-97710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-97710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi -\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi }{2})-(0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identities\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-97710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "032485b9c04d38c8796bb8cb7044f0da734fa798c5bcd90a61945b524ddeedac"} {"question" "q-97710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-97710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi +1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi -1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-97710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2)+x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=2-t+{t}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2-t+{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=2,\\,p(2)=4,\\,p(3)=8.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{84} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "032485b9c04d38c8796bb8cb7044f0da734fa798c5bcd90a61945b524ddeedac"} {"question" "q-141167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{21}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{14}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{21}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{21}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\,({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi +\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi }{2})-((-\\pi )+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identities\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "032485b9c04d38c8796bb8cb7044f0da734fa798c5bcd90a61945b524ddeedac"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2\\pi +1}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2\\pi +2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2\\pi }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNormalize the vector $f(t)=t-{t}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{(t-{t}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+{t}^{4}),\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}.\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $f(t),$ we divide $f(t)$ by its norm $‖f(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{f(t)}{‖f(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t-{t}^{2})}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}(t-{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6b641bf3cd27d887240865c5ef1c6b7f0067e291035cc5141e31bc1a5801a9"} {"question" "q-141175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067839/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "032485b9c04d38c8796bb8cb7044f0da734fa798c5bcd90a61945b524ddeedac"} {"question" "q-141175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-fi